  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 348 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nModest high pressure will be the prevailing surface feature \ntoday with a wavy quasi-stationary west-to-east front sitting \nover the Central Plains, mid-Mississippi River Valley into the \nOhio Valley. Low-level moisture remains fairly high this morning\nwith near-zero to zero dewpoint depressions across just about \nall of the WFO MPX coverage area, resulting in areas of fog over\nsouthern and eastern portions of our coverage area. Once that \nlow level moisture burns off, a mix of sun and clouds can be \nexpected with highs into the low-mid 80s. Clouds increase \ntonight as the front lifts back north while ever-present Hudson \nBay upper level low spins a potent shortwave around its western \nand southern fringes towards the Upper Midwest later tonight \ninto early Tuesday morning. Nocturnal jetting will also again \ndevelop within a fairly unstable environment, putting \ningredients in place for the development of overnight \nshowers/thunderstorms. There is already decent CAM agreement on \na cluster of storms looking to develop in eastern SD and \nnortheastern NE around 06z tonight, then pressing ESE into \nsouthwestern-southern MN, thus near and south of the MN River. \nArguments can be made of having an MCS develop if the convection\naligns with the timing/placement of the nocturnal LLJ. As such,\nthis aligns with the placement of the Marginal Risk in the \nSWODY1 (and the edge of the Slight Risk along our border with \nWFO FSD). Due to the timing of the potential convection, the \nSWODY1 bleeds into the start of the SWODY2 period, hence the \nvery similar placement of the Marginal Risk in both outlooks.\n\nPrecipitation will wane shortly after sunrise Tuesday morning, \nmaking for a tranquil day with highs again into the low-mid 80s \nand highest dewpoints pooled in far southern MN, into the lower \n70s. Another round of scattered showers looks to develop late \nTuesday night into early Wednesday morning but this round looks \nmuch more benign, potentially not even having thunderstorms \nassociated with it. \n\nOnce past this early Wednesday morning round of rain, conditions \nlook fairly dry through the rest of the workweek as high \npressure nudging southeast from western Canada will be the \nprevailing feature. So much so, in fact, that temperatures are \nstill advertised to take a drop to below normal levels from \nThursday onward, coming out of the 80s and into the 70s. There \nis a weak low pressure system over the Plains which may thrust \nsome isentropic lift into the region, potentially producing \nscattered rain showers for Friday night into Saturday but again \nnothing looks to be organized or impactful. The below normal \ntemperatures will then remain in place through the upcoming \nweekend with little in the way of appreciable precipitation.",
